{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d02c41b3aad1fefb","name":"card_event_corps_command","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/ANZAC/events/card_event_corps_command","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"CORPS COMMAND","text":"Your left and right-most cards in hand get: \"Destroy a random enemy unit when played.\"","flavorText":"During WWII, a corps headquarters coordinated the operations of several divisions, directing large formations across a broad front while managing planning, logistics, communications and the allocation of supporting forces. For Australia, I Corps became the main operational corps for much of WWII, commanding Australian and other Allied formations in several theatres. From 1940 to 1942, I Corps operated in the Mediterranean and Middle East, overseeing forces in North Africa, Greece and Syria–Lebanon. During the Greek campaign in 1941, its headquarters moved to Greece as part of Operation Lustre. Because it controlled the New Zealand 2nd Division alongside Australian, British and Greek formations, I Corps was officially renamed ANZAC Corps on 12 April, under Lieutenant General Thomas Blamey. The campaign was short-lived, and the corps headquarters was evacuated later that month. After Japan entered the war, I Corps was transferred to the South West Pacific. From late 1942 onward, it commanded Allied frontline formations in New Guinea, and it later oversaw operations in Borneo in 1945, making corps-level command central to Australia’s war effort across multiple theatres.","faction":"Anzac","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_corps_command","title":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_corps_command_title","SourceString":"CORPS COMMAND","LocalizedString":"CORPS COMMAND"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Anzac","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_corps_command_text","SourceString":"Your left and right-most cards in hand get: \"Destroy a random enemy unit when played.\"","LocalizedString":"Your left and right-most cards in hand get: \"Destroy a random enemy unit when played.\""},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_corps_command_history","SourceString":"During WWII, a corps headquarters coordinated the operations of several divisions, directing large formations across a broad front while managing planning, logistics, communications and the allocation of supporting forces. For Australia, I Corps became the main operational corps for much of WWII, commanding Australian and other Allied formations in several theatres. From 1940 to 1942, I Corps operated in the Mediterranean and Middle East, overseeing forces in North Africa, Greece and Syria–Lebanon. During the Greek campaign in 1941, its headquarters moved to Greece as part of Operation Lustre. Because it controlled the New Zealand 2nd Division alongside Australian, British and Greek formations, I Corps was officially renamed ANZAC Corps on 12 April, under Lieutenant General Thomas Blamey. The campaign was short-lived, and the corps headquarters was evacuated later that month. After Japan entered the war, I Corps was transferred to the South West Pacific. From late 1942 onward, it commanded Allied frontline formations in New Guinea, and it later oversaw operations in Borneo in 1945, making corps-level command central to Australia’s war effort across multiple theatres.","LocalizedString":"During WWII, a corps headquarters coordinated the operations of several divisions, directing large formations across a broad front while managing planning, logistics, communications and the allocation of supporting forces. For Australia, I Corps became the main operational corps for much of WWII, commanding Australian and other Allied formations in several theatres. From 1940 to 1942, I Corps operated in the Mediterranean and Middle East, overseeing forces in North Africa, Greece and Syria–Lebanon. During the Greek campaign in 1941, its headquarters moved to Greece as part of Operation Lustre. Because it controlled the New Zealand 2nd Division alongside Australian, British and Greek formations, I Corps was officially renamed ANZAC Corps on 12 April, under Lieutenant General Thomas Blamey. The campaign was short-lived, and the corps headquarters was evacuated later that month. After Japan entered the war, I Corps was transferred to the South West Pacific. From late 1942 onward, it commanded Allied frontline formations in New Guinea, and it later oversaw operations in Borneo in 1945, making corps-level command central to Australia’s war effort across multiple theatres."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","isInPermanentPool":true,"usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnCounterMeasureTriggered"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_4'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_4.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/CORPS_COMMAND.CORPS_COMMAND","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_corps_command_photo","SourceString":"Australian soldiers on parade in New Guinea, 1944.","LocalizedString":"Australian soldiers on parade in New Guinea, 1944."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"tempCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"_DoOnCardPlayedOrGotchaTriggered","owner":"card_event_corps_command_C","params":[{"name":"cardBeingPlayed","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Public | FUNC_HasDefaults | FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32